// An image library over the Gemini image model ("nano banana"): generate an image `file` from a text
// prompt, or hand it an image `file` + an instruction to get an edited image `file` back — PURE
// Katari, no sidecar. An edit's input image rides the request body as a file value in the
// `inlineData.data` slot (`http.json` base64s it only at the send boundary, so the content never
// enters the value plane); the response's first image part becomes a real `file` through
// `files.from_base64`. A reply with no image is the typed `no_image` (the model refused, or answered
// in text) — never a panic. The capability follows the credentials unification:
// `use imagegen.provider(source = credentials.env(key = "GEMINI_API_KEY"))`.
//
// Both tools are one call each above `generated_image`: the endpoint, the api-key header, the failure
// classification and the read of the reply are written ONCE there, and what stays in each tool is the
// user parts it builds. A failed call arrives as `http.api_failure` — classified by the stdlib's
// `http.classify_status`: `http.auth_error` for a 401/403 (the key is what failed; a fresh RESOLUTION
// is the recovery) and `http.api_error` for every other non-2xx — rather than as the transport's bare
// `http.status_error`, so the caller's one converter over the stdlib vocabulary covers this package
// too, and where the line falls is decided in one place for every package that draws it.
//
// The MODEL ID is provider CONFIGURATION, not a second capability: it is an argument of `provider`,
// held in its handler state, and it rides back on the ONE `credential` request together with the key
// a call authenticates with — the same shape the `ai` providers give their `connection`.

@"What one image call runs against: the @model@ id it targets, and the @api_key@ it authenticates with
(a `string of private`, protected by its taint)."
data connection(
  @"The image model id this call targets." model: string,
  @"The Gemini API key, resolved for this call." api_key: string of private,
)

@"The image-model capability — provided at the root by `use imagegen.provider(...)`. It hands back the
whole `connection`, not just the key: the model id is the provider's CONFIGURATION (an argument of
`provider`, held in its handler state), not a capability of its own, so a call learns what it is
calling and as whom in ONE request instead of two. The credential half is resolved per call, so an env
rotation or an oauth refresh lands without a restart."
request credential() -> connection

@"Provide the image-model capability for the extent of the continuation: the credential named by
@source@ (a NAME, resolved at each use) and the @model@ every call targets, served together."
agent provider[R, effect E](
  source: credentials.source,
  @"The image model id every call targets — provider configuration, held in the handler's state." model: string ?= "gemini-2.5-flash-image",
  continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, credential},
) -> R with E | io | prelude.throw[env.missing_secret | oauth.server_error] {
  use handler (var chosen = model) {
    request credential() -> connection {
      next connection(model = chosen, api_key = credentials.resolve(source = source))
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"The model answered without an image — a refusal or a text answer; @message@ carries whatever it
said. Typed, so a tool wrapper can hand the text back to the model instead of failing the run."
data no_image(message: string)

@"The first candidate's content parts of a generateContent reply, read totally — any missing layer
degrades to the empty list rather than throwing."
agent response_parts(response: unknown) -> array[unknown] {
  match (json.field(target = response, key = "candidates")) {
    case array(candidates) -> match (array.get(target = candidates, index = 0)) {
      case null -> []
      case first -> match (json.field(target = json.field(target = first, key = "content"), key = "parts")) {
        case array(parts) -> parts
        case _ -> []
      }
    }
    case _ -> []
  }
}

@"One part's inline-image MIME type, defaulting to image/png when the reply omits it."
agent part_content_type(part: unknown) -> string {
  let recorded = json.text(target = json.field(target = json.field(target = part, key = "inlineData"), key = "mimeType"))
  if (recorded == "") { "image/png" } else { recorded }
}

@"POST a generateContent turn whose user @parts@ are the caller's, and hand back the reply's parsed
document — the ONE wire call both tools make (same endpoint, same api-key header), so only the parts
differ between generating and editing.

A non-2xx arrives here as the transport's `http.status_error` and leaves as `http.api_failure`,
classified by the stdlib's `http.classify_status`: this is the one boundary where the status is read,
and above it no caller sees a bare status again. A 401 or 403 becomes `http.auth_error` — the Gemini
key is wrong, revoked, or not entitled to the image model, and a `replay` converter turns that into a
fresh resolution of the credential (and, when it cannot be refreshed, the runtime's re-authorization
park). Every other non-2xx becomes `http.api_error` — a rejected prompt, a rate limit, a model name
that does not exist — carrying the server's body verbatim, because it is the diagnosis a model reads
and adjusts to, and the STATUS onward, so a retry policy downstream reads a number. @context@ names
the call (\"image edit\", \"image generation\") on whichever variant comes out. A transport failure
(`http.fetch_error`) and a 2xx that is not JSON (`json.parse_error`) are NOT statuses to classify, so
the residual rethrows them as bound."
agent generate_content(parts: array[unknown], context: string) -> unknown {
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: http.status_error | http.fetch_error | json.parse_error) -> never {
      match (error) {
        case http.status_error(status => status, body => body) ->
          prelude.throw(error = http.classify_status(status = status, context = context, message = body))
        // The residual is `fetch_error | parse_error` — rethrown as bound, no reconstruction.
        case rest -> prelude.throw(error = rest)
      }
    }
  }
  // One request for both halves: the model this call targets and the key it authenticates with.
  let client = credential()
  http.post_json(
    url = f"https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models/${client.model}:generateContent",
    body = { contents = [{ role = "user", parts = parts }] },
    headers = { "x-goog-api-key" = client.api_key },
  )
}

@"The whole shared shape of both tools: make the generateContent call with the caller's @parts@, then
hand back the reply's first image as a `file`. What is left in each tool above it is exactly the parts
it builds — everything else (the endpoint, the header, the classification, the read of the reply) is
written once here.

A reply that carries no image part is the typed `no_image`, carrying whatever text the model sent
instead (a refusal, a clarification) — never a panic. @context@ names the call (\"image edit\",
\"image generation\") in every message this path can produce."
agent generated_image(parts: array[unknown], context: string) -> file {
  let reply_parts = response_parts(response = generate_content(parts = parts, context = context))
  // The first image part, via the flatten idiom (map each part to [file] or []).
  let images = array.flatten(target = for (let part in reply_parts) {
    next match (json.field(target = json.field(target = part, key = "inlineData"), key = "data")) {
      case string(data_text) -> [files.from_base64(content = data_text, content_type = part_content_type(part = part))]
      case _ -> []
    }
  })
  match (array.get(target = images, index = 0)) {
    case null -> {
      // No image came back — surface whatever the model said instead (a refusal, a clarification).
      let texts = array.flatten(target = for (let part in reply_parts) {
        next match (json.field(target = part, key = "text")) {
          case string(text) -> [text]
          case _ -> []
        }
      })
      let joined = string.join(parts = texts, separator = " ")
      let message = if (joined == "") { f"${context} returned no image" } else { f"${context} returned no image: ${joined}" }
      prelude.throw(error = no_image(message = message))
    }
    case produced -> produced
  }
}

// These docs name no sibling package's tool on purpose. This package cannot know which tools the
// model holding it was given, and a doc that promises one the holder lacks sends the model chasing a
// tool that is not there — which is how `send_files` kept being advertised after it was deleted.
@"Tool: edit an image with an instruction (e.g. \"make the sky a sunset\", \"remove the text\").
Pass the image's handle object — a bare {\"$katari_ref\": \"<id>\"} suffices; the result is a new image
file, whose handle you can pass to any of your tools that take a file — to look at it, or to post it.
A reply without an image throws the typed `no_image` carrying the model's text."
agent edit_image(image: file, instruction: string) -> file {
  generated_image(
    parts = [
      // The file value sits directly in the `data` slot; `http.json` base64s it at the send
      // boundary (no `read_base64`), exactly the ai package's gemini wire shape.
      { inlineData = { mimeType = files.content_type(value = image), "data" = image } },
      { text = instruction },
    ],
    context = "image edit",
  )
}

@"Tool: generate an image from a text prompt (e.g. \"a watercolor fox\", \"a minimalist mountain logo\").
The result is a new image file, whose handle you can pass to any of your tools that take a file — to look
at it, or to post it. A reply without an image throws the typed `no_image` carrying the model's text."
agent generate_image(prompt: string) -> file {
  generated_image(parts = [{ text = prompt }], context = "image generation")
}
